WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.430
- So welcome everyone to the
Asian American Cultural Center

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:13.950
and the Asian-American lecture series.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.470
It's really wonderful to
have so many of you here

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:17.580
on a Thursday afternoon.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:18.830
I know there's a lot going on

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:23.070
so we're all very grateful
for your presence today.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.940
So first, of course, I wanna thank the

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.870
Center for Race, Indigeneity,
and Transnational Migration

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.610
for supporting this
wonderful lecture series

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:33.870
and I'm just really thrilled

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.950
to be bringing back two of our alumni

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.580
from the Yale American Studies program,

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.353
Professors Simeon Man and Elizabeth Son.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.460
So I'm gonna give quick
introductions to the two of them

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.730
and then I'm gonna turn it over

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.570
to hear about their wonderful work.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.590
So Professor Man is an
assistant professor of history

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:56.370
at the University of
California at San Diego.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:58.240
He's an affiliate faculty member

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.050
of the department of ethnic studies

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:02.510
and the program in
critical gender studies.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.910
He specializes in Asian American history

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:08.030
and transnational US history
with a focus on the politics

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:09.570
of race and Empire.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:13.830
He received his PhD in American
Studies at Yale in 2012

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.160
and graduated from the
University of Washington

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.130
where he received his BA.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.930
And before joining the
faculty at UC San Diego,

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.830
he was an Andrew W Mellon
Postdoctoral fellow

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:27.400
at Northwestern from 2012 to 2014

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:31.410
and also a provost postdoctoral
scholar in the humanities

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.430
at the University of Southern California

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:36.060
and that was from 2014 to 2015.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.680
So we're gonna be hearing
about his first book,

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:40.030
Soldiering Through Empire:

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.520
Race and the Making of
the Decolonizing Pacific

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.280
which just came out

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.020
from the University of
California Press last year, 2018.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.840
It's a cultural history
of the US military in Asia

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.130
and Pacific after World War
II and the book explains

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.900
how the United States
mobilized citizens from nations

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.840
and territories throughout the region

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.390
for the US war in Vietnam.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.530
And particularly, the
work really reconsiders

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.970
this question of soldiering as labor,

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:10.402
as military labor, civilian
labor, forms of labor

00:02:10.402 --> 00:02:12.900
that's critical to the expansion

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.700
of the American Empire post-war.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:21.250
Professor Elizabeth Son will
be following Simeon Man.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.040
And she's an associate
professor, recently tenured,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.284
at Northwestern University.

00:02:26.284 --> 00:02:29.284
(audience applause)

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:33.300
She completed her American Studies PhD

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.920
a year before professor Man, in 2011,

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.980
so she has courtesy appointment...

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.060
Oh sorry, she's associate
professor of theater

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.540
at Northwestern University.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.520
She holds courtesy appointments

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.520
in the Department of Performance Studies,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.760
Asian-American Studies and the
program in American Studies.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.040
She's an interdisciplinary
scholar whose research focuses

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.620
on the interplay between
histories of gender-based violence

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.050
and contemporary performance
in the United States

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.253
and South Korea.

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:05.990
Her book, which we'll be hearing from,

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:09.380
Embodied Reckonings:
"Comfort Women," Performance,

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.360
and Transpacific Redress

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:15.470
is from the University of
Michigan Press, also 2018

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:18.450
and examines the political
and cultural aspects

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.200
of contemporary
performances in South Korea,

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.130
Japan and the United States

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:24.380
that have grappled with the history

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.770
of Japanese military sexual slavery.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:29.720
Her scholarly articles have appeared

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:33.210
in Asian Theatre Journal,
Theatre Survey, Theater

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:36.210
and many others, and she's
also a public scholar.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.160
She writes off ads which have
appeared in such publications

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:42.233
as the Los Angeles Review
of Books and The Hill.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:44.940
Both scholars have garnered awards

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:47.070
for their amazing
groundbreaking scholarship

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.510
and we're delighted to have
them back on campus today.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.480
So please join me in welcoming them

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.561
and the first to speak is Professor Man.

00:03:54.561 --> 00:03:57.561
(audience applause)

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.620
- So thank you so much
Mary and thank you everyone

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:05.453
for being here.

00:04:05.453 --> 00:04:09.410
It's really, really wonderful
to be back in the space.

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:12.690
This is where the project got started,

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:16.260
being in conversation with
so many people and my cohort,

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:20.050
in our seminars and I think
it's really interesting

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:22.580
that even though we each have

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:26.033
our own very individual
projects, I think we all,

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:31.090
we're grappling with some
broad similar questions,

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:33.370
questions that were
percolating in American Studies

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:36.550
at the time about the limits of liberalism

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:39.540
and its promise of
freedom through inclusion

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:43.030
and ways of reckoning
with histories of violence

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:45.460
and it's long persistent aftermaths.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:46.870
So Soldiering Through Empire

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:49.110
which I'm gonna talk
about today really grew

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.083
out of these conversations.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:56.310
It is an attempt to
wrestle with what I see

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.110
as one of the central paradoxes

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:01.360
of post-1945 US-Imperial culture,

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:04.540
that is how did the United
States legitimate itself

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.620
as a champion of racial
equality and democracy

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:10.340
and yet, at the same time,
expanded state powers

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:14.600
to wage war throughout
the decolonizing world?

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.190
How do we reconcile the
seeming contradiction

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:21.080
between these dueling
imperatives of liberal inclusion

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:23.510
and racialized state violence?

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:26.620
So I approached this question
by turning to the military

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.710
and exploring the ways that Asian soldiers

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:32.180
were mobilized for US military projects

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:35.663
in Asia and the Pacific,
especially during the Vietnam War.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.100
In one sense, the book is
an attempt to tell a history

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.020
of the International Vietnam war beyond

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.280
an area studies framework
of Cold War history

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.450
and to think instead about
the legacies of colonialism

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:53.440
in Asia and the Pacific and
how they continue to manifest

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:56.760
through the militarized
labor of their citizens.

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:59.350
And I look at three specific sites;

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:03.000
the Philippines, South Korea and Hawaii.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:05.790
In these countries and
territories after World War II,

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:08.830
we see a similar pattern
of colonial subjects

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:13.560
being liberated from colonialism
to take part in US war.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.650
This process of ushering
freedom through violence

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:20.640
was at the heart of US
imperialism after 1945

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.300
and I call this process the making

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.800
of the decolonizing Pacific.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.960
The decolonizing Pacific is
not so much a fixed geography

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.950
but rather names a historical conjuncture

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:32.370
in which the unfinished struggles

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.890
for decolonization throughout
Asia and the Pacific

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:38.980
became intertwined with and
co-opted by the US imperative

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:41.493
of securing the global capitalist economy.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:46.300
Asia for Asians as a
discourse and a practice

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.850
was the US response to
the emerging third-world.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:53.200
It was an attempt to pacify
anti-colonial resistance

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.550
and to channel the freedom
dreams of the formerly colonized

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:59.510
into the economy of war making.

00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:02.640
So viewed through this lens
of the decolonizing Pacific,

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:05.260
the involvements of different
nations and territories

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:07.970
in the Vietnam War tells us a larger story

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:10.670
than the International Vietnam War.

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:13.360
Instead, it reveals that
the war was the very ground

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:15.840
on which the struggles
were an unfulfilled freedom

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:20.263
from colonialism would be waged,
contained and radicalized.

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:23.590
To tell this trans-pacific
history of soldiering,

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:26.320
I turn to the military
and examined the kinds

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.490
of social relations that have facilitated

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:31.243
among racialized subjects of US Empire.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:34.890
In describing the post-1945 US empire,

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:38.300
scholars have used phrases
like empire of bases,

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.680
archipelago of empire.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.040
As useful as these are
in helping us comprehend

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.943
the extensive global
reach of the US military,

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:49.720
for the most part, they
have actually limited us

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.750
in fully understanding
how the military works.

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:55.470
So in my accounts, the
military is more than just

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:58.820
a transparent essence for the US Empire

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:01.270
whose power is seemingly self-explanatory

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:05.720
by pointing to its size,
permanence or hidden nature.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:09.280
Instead, I approach the military
as a site of contingency

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:12.300
where state power operates
in far more intricate ways,

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.560
blurring the boundaries between
inclusion and exclusion,

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:18.573
violence and benevolence,
peacetime and wartime.

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:21.820
In Asian-American
Studies, the military also

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:24.690
has been treated in somewhat
one-dimensional terms

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:26.810
as an institution that has served

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:30.210
to incorporate ethnic
minorities into the nation.

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:32.790
This myopic view has
reproduced liberal narratives

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:35.290
of race and citizenship, of the transition

00:08:35.290 --> 00:08:38.000
from racial exclusion
to national inclusion

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.070
with World War II marking
the turning points.

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:43.790
So my examination of
Asian-American racial formation

00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:45.800
through a trans-pacific lens,

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:48.690
specifically through the history
of Asians being conscripted

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.840
to work the US military
Empire, there's an attempt

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.850
to disrupt this narrative.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:57.730
So my argument is this, that
Asian-American citizenship

00:08:57.730 --> 00:09:01.000
has a genealogy in US Imperial violence.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.560
Their inclusion into the
nation was predicated

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:06.380
on their mobilization
to police new boundaries

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:09.423
of exclusion elsewhere in
the decolonizing world.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:12.257
So that's the crux of the larger projects

00:09:12.257 --> 00:09:15.750
and what I'm gonna do now is
to dive into parts of the book

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:17.863
to give you a sense of that archives.

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:27.470
One of the early participants
of the US war in Vietnam

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:29.880
was this group of Filipinos mobilized

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:32.560
under the name Operation Brotherhood.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:34.810
They were doctors and
nurses who had received

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:38.360
their medical training in
US colonial institutions

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.630
and whose skills and
desires would get channeled

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:44.890
into the service of the
post-world War II US empire.

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:48.010
The first contingent shown
here arrived in South Vietnam

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:51.070
in the fall of 1954,
soon after the collapse

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:52.290
of French colonial rule

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:54.713
and the Geneva Accords
divided the country.

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:58.850
Specifically, they were
mobilized by the CIA

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.710
as an experiment in psychological warfare.

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:04.740
Their task was to win the
trust of the Vietnamese people

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:05.940
and to steer them closer

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:08.300
to the South Vietnamese governments.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:11.330
The CIA refer to them
in racializing terms.

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:14.090
They saw them as
quote-unquote, free Asians

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:15.990
who could demonstrate to the Vietnamese;

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:18.660
quote, from one brown-skin to another

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:22.240
what it meant to embody liberal democracy.

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:25.730
If the Philippines was the show
window of American democracy

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:28.150
as they were popularly known at the time

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:30.000
in Asia, during the cold war,

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.600
the Filipinos of Operation
Brotherhood helped convey

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:34.853
that idea through counterinsurgency.

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:40.960
Wait, so here is an image
of Dr. Antonio Velasco

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:44.950
with this first contingent and this is

00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:49.737
actually the kinds of images
that the CIA wanted to project

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:54.270
of Asians helping Asians,
with Americans receding

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:57.873
in the background or at most,
taking a secondary role.

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:02.020
So to Americans, Operation
Brotherhood was a means

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:05.650
to an end of winning and
securing the population.

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:08.400
The actual medical remedy
they provided the Vietnamese

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.350
was in many ways inconsequential

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:14.430
and they actually openly
admitted that in their reports.

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:17.580
One report written by
Edward Lansdale is revealing

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:18.990
in the sense.

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:21.910
So in the report, he noted that
besides their medical work,

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:24.870
the Filipinos also performed
a kind of social intimacy

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:26.840
with the Vietnamese that was crucial

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:28.890
to the counterinsurgency.

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:31.150
Quote, they made up their own songs,

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:34.360
they held parties in off-duty
hours and in general,

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:37.000
were a real tonic to the dispirited.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:40.010
Even the presence of pretty
Filipino girl doctors

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:42.840
was enough to encourage
Vietnamese to learn English

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:44.923
so they could talk to them, end quote.

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:49.180
So there's a picture of Edward Lansdale

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:52.883
being kind of creepy, just
standing in the background.

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:58.820
So Edward Lansdale figures quite centrally

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:00.180
in most historical accounts

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:03.340
of the Philippines-Vietnam connection

00:12:03.340 --> 00:12:05.450
because he was the one who was credited

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:09.190
with perfecting counterinsurgency
tactics in the Philippines

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:12.350
and then exporting it to South Vietnam.

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:15.100
Historians have rightly argued
that we need to move away

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:18.230
from Lansdale because he has a tendency

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:22.260
of over-emphasizing his own
influence but at the same time,

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:24.400
we turned to his papers and his archives

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:26.880
because I think his documents reveal

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:30.030
what I'm calling the intimacies
of counterinsurgency,

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:34.260
these kinds of social relations
that are otherwise occluded

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:38.143
if we only focus on
military state documents.

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:43.300
So one of the nurses, Josefina Pablo,

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:44.840
recalled in an interview.

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:46.567
She said, "The Vietnamese trusted us

00:12:46.567 --> 00:12:48.760
"and accepted us very well."

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.130
And it was this trust that
made it possible for her

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:53.223
to teach them about public health.

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:56.300
Josefina was 25 years old when she arrived

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:58.640
in South Vietnam in 1955.

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:00.040
She was a recent graduate

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:02.580
of St. Luke's Hospital School of Nursing

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:04.370
which was established by Americans

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:05.993
during the colonial period.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:09.700
Josefina saw Operation Brotherhood
as fulfilling her desire

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:12.490
to quote, travel and see the world

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:15.180
and to put to good use her nursing degree.

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:17.810
She had no expectations
of securing South Vietnam

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:21.800
from communism, that figured
far in the back of her mind.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:24.470
Instead, she simply
considered her medical deeds

00:13:24.470 --> 00:13:25.790
on behalf of the Vietnamese

00:13:25.790 --> 00:13:28.610
her most lasting accomplishments.

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:31.130
So this is the point
that I wanna emphasize,

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:34.350
although these Filipinos
traveled to South Vietnam

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:37.240
as educated subjects of the US Empire,

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:40.260
just as Americans had
intended, their interactions

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:42.060
with the Vietnamese, at times, testified

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:45.070
to a colonial intimacy
of a different kind,

00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:47.630
an affection that refused
to be instrumentalized

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:51.000
and that at least momentarily
signaled something else beyond

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:54.000
the counterinsurgency imperative.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:56.500
At the same time, that
it's important to recognize

00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:58.190
that their desires became channeled

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:00.690
into the work of counterinsurgency.

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:03.190
We also need to recognize
that the US project

00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:06.250
of securing Asia for
Asians sowed the seeds

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:11.023
of its own unraveling as we'll
see in a different example.

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:15.820
So I'm gonna turn now
to, briefly, to Hawaii

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:18.400
because in Hawaii, we
also see the entanglements

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:20.960
of liberalism and state violence at work

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:23.290
but also the challenge posed by anti-war

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:25.900
and anti colonial resistance.

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:29.100
Like Philippine-Independence,
Hawaii statehood in 1959

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:31.770
was hailed as a gift to
freedom demonstrating

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:34.720
the US commitment to liberal democracy

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:37.140
and similar to how
Filipinos were recruited

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:39.460
to extend that gift to the Vietnamese,

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:42.450
discourses of Hawaii's
statehoods were mobilized

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:44.163
in the service of war.

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:50.340
In 1965, the 25th Division
known as Hawaii's Own deployed

00:14:50.510 --> 00:14:53.480
from Schofield Barracks in
Oahu to Cu Chi districts

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:55.250
in South Vietnam.

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:57.850
Its mission was standard
counterinsurgency,

00:14:57.850 --> 00:15:00.160
soldiers built schools and hospitals.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:02.180
They launched public health campaigns

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:04.810
and generally sought to
convey their good intentions

00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:05.943
to the people.

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:08.770
As part of their operation,

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:12.200
the soldiers collected
donations from Hawaii citizens

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:14.180
to give to the Vietnamese.

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:16.810
The operation was called Helping Hand

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:19.720
and the entire spectacle
was replete with references

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:21.600
to Hawaii statehood.

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:23.930
The commanding general
called it an extension

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:26.690
of our aloha to the people of Vietnam

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:28.930
and like Operation Brotherhood,

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:31.770
Operation Helping Hand
succeeded in large part

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:35.020
by enacting different stories
about different places,

00:15:35.020 --> 00:15:37.990
folding one narrative into another.

00:15:37.990 --> 00:15:40.700
If statehood was celebrated
as the liberal inclusion

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:43.060
of Hawaii into the US nation,

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:44.720
then that became the narrative frame

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:46.740
for the counterinsurgency

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:49.380
as the soldier sought to
incorporate the Vietnamese

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:52.140
into a vision of domesticity
that was predicated

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:54.810
on the ongoing dispossession
of Native Hawaiians

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:56.343
of their land and sovereigncy.

00:15:57.950 --> 00:15:59.600
Such liberal enactments of war

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:02.957
could not ultimately contain
the war's brutal violence

00:16:02.957 --> 00:16:06.330
and that violence was not the
result of counterinsurgency

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:10.610
gone awry but the logical
extension of liberal warfare.

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:14.900
So in the book, I discussed
some of these examples

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:19.900
of the kinds of racialized
training that took place in Oahu,

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:24.820
notably, so at Schofield Barracks,

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:27.190
they created a mock Vietnamese village

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:31.960
where soldiers trained and
I've uncovered a connection

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:33.793
between the soldiers who trained here

00:16:33.793 --> 00:16:37.430
and the My Lai massacre
that took place in 1968.

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:39.690
So my point in making these connections

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:42.897
is not simply to draw a causal connection

00:16:42.897 --> 00:16:44.520
but to make the broader argument

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:47.280
that liberal inclusion
produces the condition

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:49.990
for state violence and it's disavowal.

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:53.190
Racial liberalism was never
simply a cold war imperative

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:56.060
that occasionally gets forestalled by war.

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:57.960
Instead war was a terrain upon

00:16:57.960 --> 00:17:00.600
which racial liberalism gained traction

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:02.453
in the post-World War II period.

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:07.633
So I'm gonna wrap up by
discussing just one more image.

00:17:08.600 --> 00:17:13.600
So this image, a map of Oahu appeared in,

00:17:13.840 --> 00:17:17.890
was published in an underground
anti-war GI publication,

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:21.563
called Liberated Barracks
based in Schofield Barracks.

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:26.520
It introduced a critical
reading of Oahu's geography,

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:29.320
mimicking a tourist
map and guiding readers

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:31.850
to the less familiar sites,

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:34.860
than what is presented
usually in a tourist brochure.

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:37.660
So in the top-left corner,
you can't really read it,

00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:40.210
it says under scenic
points of interest stated,

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:43.900
be sure to visit all US military
occupied lands indicated

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:45.490
by blacked-out areas

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:50.150
And one of these blacked-out
areas was the My Lai,

00:17:50.150 --> 00:17:53.173
what they called the My
Lai Jungle Training Center.

00:17:55.250 --> 00:17:58.163
So pointing the reader's gaze
to the middle of the island,

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:01.690
the text revealed Hawaii's little Vietnam

00:18:01.690 --> 00:18:05.110
as the genesis of the 1968 massacre.

00:18:05.110 --> 00:18:08.140
The point of the map
was really quite simple,

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:11.230
to make transparent the
processes of militarization

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:14.410
that had gone hand in hand
with Hawaii's transformation

00:18:14.410 --> 00:18:16.780
into a so-called paradise.

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:18.910
That these occurred in
lockstep was to justify

00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:20.590
these final words at the bottom,

00:18:20.590 --> 00:18:23.773
Paradise in the Pacific
for Genocide in Indochina.

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:28.840
This map represents the
kind of radical critiques

00:18:28.840 --> 00:18:32.120
against the war and US Empire
that were emerging in Hawaii

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:34.870
during this period and on the one hand,

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:37.990
it would be easy for us
to interpret this activism

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:42.990
as a peripheral part of the
mainstream US anti-war movements

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:48.040
but if we expand our
view and situate our view

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:50.810
beyond the US framework,
it becomes apparent

00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:53.210
that the struggles in
Hawaii were actually part

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:57.510
of the growing insurgencies
of the decolonizing Pacific.

00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:01.580
In the Philippines, Okinawa,
Japan and elsewhere,

00:19:01.580 --> 00:19:05.193
anti-military movements
were ascendant in the 1960s.

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:09.150
Base workers, American GIs,

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:12.720
labor and anti-war
organizers forged critical

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:16.210
and difficult alliances in
an effort to end the war

00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:18.610
in Vietnam and their
governments complicity

00:19:18.610 --> 00:19:21.130
with US imperialism.

00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:23.540
These were all subjects of the US Empire

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:26.230
who were radicalized by
the war and struggled

00:19:26.230 --> 00:19:28.020
for a different conception of freedom

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:30.200
that did not require their participation

00:19:30.200 --> 00:19:31.853
in US state violence.

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:34.750
So I end the book with these accounts

00:19:34.750 --> 00:19:37.430
of these trans-pacific movements

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:41.320
in order to show how US
militarism in Asia and the Pacific

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:43.510
and the broader US imperial projects

00:19:43.510 --> 00:19:45.780
of securing an Asia for Asians

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:47.350
had the unintended consequence

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:50.810
of radicalizing the unfinished
struggles for decolonization

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:52.940
that were foreclosed.

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:54.987
I'll end there, thank you.

00:19:54.987 --> 00:19:57.987
(audience applause)

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:25.580
- I wanna start off by thanking Mary

00:20:25.580 --> 00:20:28.240
and the Center for the
Study of Race, Indigeneity

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:31.590
and Transnational Migration
for this invitation.

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:34.450
It's wonderful to share
the floor with Simeon,

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:37.980
to meet so many students working
in Asian-American Studies,

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:40.750
to see many of my teachers in the room

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:44.023
and to celebrate ER&amp;M's 20th anniversary.

00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:48.540
Almost 12 years ago,
when I began my research

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:51.720
on transnational activism
surrounding the history

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:54.320
of Japanese military sexual slavery,

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:58.370
I asked elderly Korean survivors
of wartime sexual violence

00:20:58.370 --> 00:21:03.110
why they attended the
27-year-long weekly protests held

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:07.080
across from the Japanese
Embassy in Seoul, South Korea.

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:09.610
One woman, Kil-wa-nok
(speaks foreign language)

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:12.170
the word for grandmother in Korean,

00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:14.890
explained that it was for the future.

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:17.000
As a living witness she believed

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:19.640
that it was her responsibility
to tell the world

00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:22.963
about her experiences
and to fight for justice.

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:26.890
Kil-wa-nok was a teenager in 1940

00:21:26.890 --> 00:21:29.350
when she was deceived
into becoming a sex slave

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:32.410
for Japanese troops in northeast China.

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:36.080
Like Kil-wa-nok, girl and young
women from Japan's colonies

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:39.670
and occupied territories
endured unspeakable violence

00:21:39.670 --> 00:21:43.650
during their captivity in what
were essentially, rape camps.

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:46.020
These so-called comfort
stations could be found

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:49.440
throughout Japan's colonies
and occupied territories

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:52.300
in China, the Philippines, Southeast Asia

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:53.930
and the Pacific island.

00:21:53.930 --> 00:21:55.890
For almost five decades,

00:21:55.890 --> 00:21:59.100
personal and public silence
shrouded this history

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:03.200
until Korean survivors began
coming forward in the 1990s

00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:06.310
and galvanized the then
nascent social movement

00:22:06.310 --> 00:22:07.403
for redress.

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:11.300
Activists have pursued
political and legal redress

00:22:11.300 --> 00:22:12.330
that has centered

00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:14.370
on the Japanese
government's acknowledgment

00:22:14.370 --> 00:22:18.210
of Japanese military sexual
slavery and official apology

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:19.750
and reparations.

00:22:19.750 --> 00:22:22.260
Alongside the work of activists,

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:25.070
artists have been using
various cultural forms

00:22:25.070 --> 00:22:28.180
from literature and visual
art to the performing arts

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:29.720
to engage with the history

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:32.460
of Japanese military sexual slavery.

00:22:32.460 --> 00:22:35.590
To this day, the Japanese
government continues

00:22:35.590 --> 00:22:38.800
to deny survivors full
reparative measures,

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:41.510
despite the agreement in 2015

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:44.420
between the governments
of South Korea and Japan

00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:48.400
to try to quote, resolve,
end quote the issue.

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:51.890
In response to a long
history of state denials

00:22:51.890 --> 00:22:54.880
and half-hearted apologetic innuendos,

00:22:54.880 --> 00:22:58.800
a vibrant transnational
network of local activists

00:22:58.800 --> 00:23:02.963
has organized throughout Asia,
Europe and the United States.

00:23:04.230 --> 00:23:06.950
In my book, I show how
the official erasure

00:23:06.950 --> 00:23:09.650
of wartime sexual crimes in which Japan

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:12.430
and the United States
participated after the war

00:23:12.430 --> 00:23:15.570
and Japanese State denials
of official redress,

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:17.010
inadvertently nurtured

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:22.010
a transnational Asian/American
movement of activism

00:23:22.090 --> 00:23:24.500
and artistic intervention.

00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:28.100
I argue that performance
plays a central role

00:23:28.100 --> 00:23:32.360
in how survivors, activists
and artists have grappled

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:35.700
with histories of sexual
violence and the erasures

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:37.690
of such experiences.

00:23:37.690 --> 00:23:40.540
The history of Japanese
military sexual slavery,

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:43.340
a history that centered on the violation

00:23:43.340 --> 00:23:46.840
of women's bodies calls for representing,

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:49.840
mobilizing and reinvesting those bodies

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:53.540
with meaning through redressive
acts such as protests,

00:23:53.540 --> 00:23:57.400
tribunals, theater and memorial building.

00:23:57.400 --> 00:23:58.850
The book builds on the work

00:23:58.850 --> 00:24:01.710
of transnational
Asian-American Studies scholars

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:04.800
like Lisa Yoneyama and Cathy Schlund-Vials

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:07.770
in extending Lisa Lowe's
framework of the quote,

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:12.480
tireless reckoning, end quote
with America's imperial past

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:16.720
to trans-pacific cultural
practices of memory and justice.

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:18.610
In the case of the comfort women history,

00:24:18.610 --> 00:24:20.530
this reckoning has to contend

00:24:20.530 --> 00:24:23.620
with what Yoneyama calls the quote,

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:28.180
trans-pacific arrangement of
cold war justice, end quote.

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:31.170
Japanese state denials of
the comfort women history

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:35.220
tell only part of the
multinational silencing

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:37.650
of Japanese wartime criminality.

00:24:37.650 --> 00:24:39.750
The United States is implicated

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:43.360
in official post-war
erasures of sexual war crimes

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:45.540
during the Tokyo war crimes trials,

00:24:45.540 --> 00:24:49.260
inadequate post-war settlements
for Japanese war crimes

00:24:49.260 --> 00:24:53.410
and involvement in the afterlife
of militarized sexual labor

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:56.310
and violence in Japan and South Korea

00:24:56.310 --> 00:24:59.500
from 1945 to the present.

00:24:59.500 --> 00:25:02.320
By focusing on live public enactments

00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:05.870
and interactions between
performers and audiences,

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:09.230
I probe more deeply into
the embodied aspects

00:25:09.230 --> 00:25:13.470
of remembrance and how
the everyday of activism

00:25:13.470 --> 00:25:17.660
and artistic work can become
a sight a vibrant change

00:25:17.660 --> 00:25:22.210
that confronts the amnesia
of transnational politics.

00:25:22.210 --> 00:25:25.080
The public embodiment of
memory and its relation

00:25:25.080 --> 00:25:28.910
to community formation
are important to my study.

00:25:28.910 --> 00:25:32.060
The women's acts of
remembrance are central

00:25:32.060 --> 00:25:34.310
to their public activism.

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:38.270
Communities of remembrance are
also sustained and nurtured

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:41.070
by the involvement of non-state actors

00:25:41.070 --> 00:25:44.550
in activism, theater
and memorial building.

00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:46.600
As Viet Nguyen writes; quote,

00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:49.660
given the scale of so
many historical traumas,

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:53.180
it can only be the case
that for many survivors,

00:25:53.180 --> 00:25:57.540
witnesses and inheritors,
the past can only be worked

00:25:57.540 --> 00:26:01.400
through together, in
collectivity and community,

00:26:01.400 --> 00:26:04.780
in struggle and solidarity, end quote.

00:26:04.780 --> 00:26:05.613
Nguyen draws attention to the necessity

00:26:05.613 --> 00:26:10.613
of a transgenerational
accounting of historical traumas

00:26:10.810 --> 00:26:14.960
for many survivors,
witnesses and inheritors.

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:17.610
Communities of remembrance
may be disparate

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:21.450
in their identification,
politics and strategies

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:25.080
yet they are connected by
the actions of remembrance

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:27.750
in the pursuit of justice.

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:29.940
For the rest of my
talk, I'm going to focus

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:33.740
on the redressive potential of
memorial building by Koreans

00:26:33.740 --> 00:26:37.533
and Korean-Americans in Seoul
and Glendale, California.

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:43.700
In recent years, as more of the
elderly survivors pass away,

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:47.090
activists are focusing on
memorialization efforts.

00:26:47.090 --> 00:26:50.660
Stone and bronze
memorials to comfort women

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:52.820
can be found all over the world.

00:26:52.820 --> 00:26:57.560
Locations range from Manila,
the Miyako Islands in Japan,

00:26:57.560 --> 00:27:00.810
Palisades Park, New Jersey,
Glendale, California,

00:27:00.810 --> 00:27:04.393
Toronto, Canada, Nanjing to Sydney.

00:27:05.260 --> 00:27:09.030
Seoul is home to the
original bronze girl statue,

00:27:09.030 --> 00:27:12.320
the most well-known comfort women memorial

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:14.270
and the majority of bronze girl statues

00:27:14.270 --> 00:27:17.620
have been installed in
South Korean cities.

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:20.527
Outside of South Korea,
the largest concentration

00:27:20.527 --> 00:27:24.350
of comfort women memorials
is in the United States.

00:27:24.350 --> 00:27:27.200
Glendale is home to the
first bronze girl statue

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:29.390
outside of Korea.

00:27:29.390 --> 00:27:32.110
And I'm interested in embodied engagements

00:27:32.110 --> 00:27:35.920
with these memorials and what
these performances can tell us

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:39.800
about the interrelationship
between sexual violence,

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:43.463
memory, performance and
transnational politics.

00:27:44.850 --> 00:27:47.300
These bronze girl memorials are examples

00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:50.440
of what Robin Bernstein
calls scriptive things.

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:53.560
She writes, quote, a scriptive
thing like a play script,

00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:57.450
broadly structures a performance
while allowing for agency

00:27:57.450 --> 00:28:00.550
and unleashing original live variations

00:28:00.550 --> 00:28:04.320
that may not be individually
predictable, end quote.

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:06.760
In the case of comfort women memorials,

00:28:06.760 --> 00:28:10.640
as scriptive things, they
invite not just individual

00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:13.370
but collective engagement
in the ethical work

00:28:13.370 --> 00:28:16.680
of remembering histories
of sexual violence.

00:28:16.680 --> 00:28:19.040
I look at memorials as scriptive things

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:22.130
that invite what I call
performances of care

00:28:22.130 --> 00:28:26.140
or embodied acts that
materialize concern and interest

00:28:26.140 --> 00:28:28.750
by providing for the needs of or looking

00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:33.130
after what one is caring
for, in this case, memorials.

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:36.620
The materiality design
and location of memorials,

00:28:36.620 --> 00:28:40.480
shape how they prompt or
invite behaviors of care

00:28:40.480 --> 00:28:42.490
as scriptive things.

00:28:42.490 --> 00:28:45.660
Dressed in a hanbok a
traditional Korean dress,

00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:49.040
the realistically rendered
barefoot bronzed adolescent,

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:51.850
sits next to an empty bronze chair.

00:28:51.850 --> 00:28:54.570
The bronze girl sits
stoically with clenched fists

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:55.880
and closed lips.

00:28:55.880 --> 00:28:58.300
She stares straight ahead
at the Japanese embassy

00:28:58.300 --> 00:29:01.610
in an accusatory manner,
though one could also read

00:29:01.610 --> 00:29:05.580
a sense of peace and self
position in her stillness.

00:29:05.580 --> 00:29:08.910
Her body language conveys
defiance and confidence.

00:29:08.910 --> 00:29:11.740
Her head is held high
and her back is straight.

00:29:11.740 --> 00:29:14.710
The statue's shadow is
laid out in a mosaic

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:17.610
of flat gray granite stones behind her

00:29:17.610 --> 00:29:20.470
that outlines the silhouette
of a seated woman.

00:29:20.470 --> 00:29:24.263
The bent back and bun of the
silhouette mark her as elderly.

00:29:26.422 --> 00:29:28.980
The statue is located on
a sidewalk directly across

00:29:28.980 --> 00:29:31.780
from the front gates of the
Japanese Embassy in Seoul

00:29:31.780 --> 00:29:35.010
where the weekly Wednesday
demonstrations take place.

00:29:35.010 --> 00:29:37.950
The location of the statue
allows multiple audiences

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:40.570
to view the statue; the Korean public,

00:29:40.570 --> 00:29:43.210
the international public
and the Japanese state.

00:29:43.210 --> 00:29:47.000
The artist who created the statue

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:49.670
and the South Korean
public colloquially refer

00:29:49.670 --> 00:29:51.920
to the statue as the
(speaks foreign language)

00:29:51.920 --> 00:29:53.253
or Girl Statue.

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:56.170
In naming her The Girl Statue,

00:29:56.170 --> 00:29:58.820
supporters anthropomorphize the memorial.

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:01.860
They refer to the statue
as her and perform actions

00:30:01.860 --> 00:30:04.100
of caring for it by dressing the statue

00:30:04.100 --> 00:30:07.970
when it is cold or leaving gifts
and letters for the statue.

00:30:07.970 --> 00:30:10.250
The act of sitting by the statue's side,

00:30:10.250 --> 00:30:13.100
wrapping clothes around
it or leaving gifts,

00:30:13.100 --> 00:30:15.960
literalizes keeping alive
the issue and memory

00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:19.710
of these women while giving
people a chance to perform care

00:30:19.710 --> 00:30:22.283
and to demonstrate their public support.

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:25.507
And this is a collage of on the top,

00:30:25.507 --> 00:30:29.100
the different outfits of
the bronze girl statue

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:31.080
and in the lower right hand corner,

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:33.740
you'll see the statue
in different positions.

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:35.750
So these statues in various positions

00:30:35.750 --> 00:30:38.780
are throughout South
Korea and it's fascinating

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:41.880
to see the ways in which the
public finds creative means

00:30:41.880 --> 00:30:44.563
to care for the bronze girl.

00:30:47.800 --> 00:30:49.520
The bronze girl statue's presence

00:30:49.520 --> 00:30:52.730
has also rescripted the
Wednesday demonstrations.

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:54.420
In the summer of 2007,

00:30:54.420 --> 00:30:57.660
when I first participated
in the protest, on average,

00:30:57.660 --> 00:31:01.040
eight to 10 survivors
attended the demonstrations.

00:31:01.040 --> 00:31:03.960
Last summer, when I attended
the Wednesday demonstration,

00:31:03.960 --> 00:31:06.730
only one survivor was in attendance.

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:10.240
She sat not too far away
from the bronze girl statue,

00:31:10.240 --> 00:31:14.670
which may soon be the only
survivor presence at the protest.

00:31:14.670 --> 00:31:17.070
The bronze girl's statue, as an effigy,

00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:20.190
as defined by Joseph Roach,
quote, fills by means

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:23.510
of segregation, a vacancy
created by the absence

00:31:23.510 --> 00:31:25.800
of any original, end quote.

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:28.340
While also marking the impossibility

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:31.330
of completely filling that absence.

00:31:31.330 --> 00:31:34.640
Moreover, the memorial as
scriptive thing invites

00:31:34.640 --> 00:31:37.890
and reminds the public of
their role in remembering

00:31:37.890 --> 00:31:41.060
the women in continuing their legacy.

00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:45.140
The presence of the bronze girl
alongside the lone survivor,

00:31:45.140 --> 00:31:48.600
powerfully highlights that we
are at a critical juncture.

00:31:48.600 --> 00:31:51.250
There is an urgent need to transfer memory

00:31:51.250 --> 00:31:53.190
to the next generation.

00:31:53.190 --> 00:31:55.800
As part of that transmission of memory,

00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:57.610
Korean-American communities

00:31:57.610 --> 00:32:00.560
are increasingly turning
to memorial projects

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:02.963
as powerful redressive acts.

00:32:04.450 --> 00:32:08.400
On July 30th, 2013 hundreds
of mostly Korean-Americans

00:32:08.400 --> 00:32:12.060
and local non-Korean politicians
gathered in Central Park

00:32:12.060 --> 00:32:15.390
in Glendale to unveil the
first comfort women memorial

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:19.470
built on public land on the
west coast of the United States.

00:32:19.470 --> 00:32:22.380
Organizers of the Korean-American
forum of California

00:32:22.380 --> 00:32:25.800
worked with Glendale City Council members

00:32:25.800 --> 00:32:28.060
to build this memorial which was funded

00:32:28.060 --> 00:32:30.460
by members of Korean-American communities

00:32:30.460 --> 00:32:32.580
in the Los Angeles area.

00:32:32.580 --> 00:32:35.910
Different Korean-American
Civic Organizations across

00:32:35.910 --> 00:32:39.140
the states have taken up
the cause of comfort women

00:32:39.140 --> 00:32:42.100
and installed memorials
in their communities.

00:32:42.100 --> 00:32:45.120
While these memorials offer
the public an opportunity

00:32:45.120 --> 00:32:48.330
to remember survivors and
their fight for justice,

00:32:48.330 --> 00:32:51.210
they are also examples of how the appeal

00:32:51.210 --> 00:32:55.130
of the comfort women issue
mobilizes political energy

00:32:55.130 --> 00:32:58.150
for other causes in
Korean-American communities

00:32:58.150 --> 00:33:00.726
such as building political alliances,

00:33:00.726 --> 00:33:05.726
increasing a voter base or
expressing a group identity.

00:33:05.890 --> 00:33:08.630
For many Korean-Americans,
commemorative practices

00:33:08.630 --> 00:33:12.760
express a trans-pacific affinity
for the comfort women cause

00:33:12.760 --> 00:33:16.800
that is truly rooted in a
belief in social justice.

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:18.780
Others see the memorials as a way

00:33:18.780 --> 00:33:21.950
to express Korean nationalistic claims

00:33:21.950 --> 00:33:25.280
or to assert their
identity as a US citizen.

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:28.870
The lines are not always
clearly demarcated

00:33:28.870 --> 00:33:31.160
between these different and sometimes,

00:33:31.160 --> 00:33:33.143
problematic motivations.

00:33:33.980 --> 00:33:36.180
The dedication ceremony in Glendale

00:33:36.180 --> 00:33:38.490
was a carefully orchestrated event

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:42.760
that helped frame the memorial
as a non-partisan issue

00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:46.040
that cuts across national
and ethnic differences.

00:33:46.040 --> 00:33:48.863
And I'm gonna focus on
one particular speaker.

00:33:50.260 --> 00:33:53.550
Kathy Masaoka of Nikkei
for Civil Rights &amp; Redress,

00:33:53.550 --> 00:33:56.920
discussed her group's
10-year grassroots campaign

00:33:56.920 --> 00:34:00.120
to win redress from the US
government for the internment

00:34:00.120 --> 00:34:03.010
of Japanese-Americans during World War II.

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:05.390
Expressing her organization's support

00:34:05.390 --> 00:34:07.320
for the comfort women survivors,

00:34:07.320 --> 00:34:09.450
she voiced the importance of an apology

00:34:09.450 --> 00:34:13.540
and reparations, "When
Japan sincerely apologizes

00:34:14.827 --> 00:34:17.637
"and pays its reparations
to each of the comfort women

00:34:17.637 --> 00:34:20.987
"before it's too late, it
will help these survivors heal

00:34:20.987 --> 00:34:24.750
"and show that Japan has
learned from its past,"

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:26.010
said Masako.

00:34:26.010 --> 00:34:28.567
Quote, she continues, "This
monument for the comfort women

00:34:28.567 --> 00:34:32.087
"has also reminded all
of us that the abuse

00:34:32.087 --> 00:34:35.647
"and trafficking of women
into forced prostitution

00:34:35.647 --> 00:34:38.647
"or domestic slavery continues today

00:34:38.647 --> 00:34:40.377
"even in this country."

00:34:41.330 --> 00:34:43.170
She placed the Comfort Women Memorial

00:34:43.170 --> 00:34:45.890
within the context of a larger discourse

00:34:45.890 --> 00:34:49.100
on violence against women,
noting that it happens

00:34:49.100 --> 00:34:51.340
even in this country.

00:34:51.340 --> 00:34:53.140
Her comments raised an important question

00:34:53.140 --> 00:34:55.910
about what is forgotten
when the experience

00:34:55.910 --> 00:34:58.820
of comfort women are remembered,

00:34:58.820 --> 00:35:01.250
through memorials like
the one in Glendale.

00:35:01.250 --> 00:35:04.660
The plaque for the memorial
invokes the never again mantra

00:35:04.660 --> 00:35:07.690
common to memorials, to mass violence.

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:09.030
Quote, and this is from the plaque,

00:35:09.030 --> 00:35:12.500
it is our sincere hope that
these unconscionable violations

00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:15.980
of human rights shall
never recur, end quote.

00:35:15.980 --> 00:35:18.900
However, there is no
mention during the ceremony

00:35:18.900 --> 00:35:21.540
of how these violations of human rights

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:25.010
might be occurring in the
sex trafficking of laborers

00:35:25.010 --> 00:35:28.960
into the Los Angeles area or
in the sex interest industries,

00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:32.040
near US military bases in Asia.

00:35:32.040 --> 00:35:34.330
Perhaps these absences are invoked

00:35:34.330 --> 00:35:38.100
in the empty chair situated on US soil.

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:41.750
As Lisa Yoneyama writes, quote,
the marked absence signified

00:35:41.750 --> 00:35:44.380
by the empty chair
placed next to the statue

00:35:44.380 --> 00:35:48.660
of the girl unsettled viewers
by reminding us other injuries

00:35:48.660 --> 00:35:52.650
from state violence that
remain equally unseen

00:35:52.650 --> 00:35:54.343
and unheard, end quote.

00:35:55.560 --> 00:35:57.230
Survivor Kim-Bok-Dong speech

00:35:57.230 --> 00:36:00.530
was the most powerful
performance I witnessed that day,

00:36:00.530 --> 00:36:02.000
dressed in a hanbok

00:36:02.000 --> 00:36:03.550
with a white (speaks foreing language) top

00:36:03.550 --> 00:36:06.850
and black Chima skirt, she
was the living embodiment

00:36:06.850 --> 00:36:08.470
of the bronze girl.

00:36:08.470 --> 00:36:13.260
Her dress in the style and
color of young girls' outfits

00:36:13.260 --> 00:36:16.150
during the time that she was
taken was both a reminder

00:36:16.150 --> 00:36:19.490
of her age when she was
forced to become a sex slave

00:36:19.490 --> 00:36:24.210
and a powerful gesture a
reclamation of that lost time.

00:36:24.210 --> 00:36:27.790
Elegantly clad and speaking
in clear steady words,

00:36:27.790 --> 00:36:31.230
she declared quote, as
I've been traveling,

00:36:31.230 --> 00:36:35.210
I have saying that if
Japan does not come forward

00:36:35.210 --> 00:36:38.810
and apologize then I
will go around the world

00:36:38.810 --> 00:36:41.860
and build memorials, end quote.

00:36:41.860 --> 00:36:44.480
Building a memorial is
both a call for redress

00:36:44.480 --> 00:36:47.130
and an act of redress itself.

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:48.057
She continues to say,

00:36:48.057 --> 00:36:52.127
"The memorial is representation
or the past history

00:36:52.127 --> 00:36:55.737
"so I ask you to try
to protect and maintain

00:36:55.737 --> 00:36:57.737
"the peace memorial here."

00:36:58.850 --> 00:37:01.530
She invoked the language of protection

00:37:01.530 --> 00:37:04.650
in extending an invitation
for others to join her

00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:06.360
in her fight for justice.

00:37:06.360 --> 00:37:09.760
She also articulated a
broad horizon of peace

00:37:09.760 --> 00:37:12.863
that will ultimately be
for future generations.

00:37:13.830 --> 00:37:15.650
The day after the dedication ceremony,

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:17.040
I met the O family.

00:37:17.040 --> 00:37:19.460
They were from Riverside
and had driven to Glendale

00:37:19.460 --> 00:37:21.530
to see the bronze girl statue

00:37:21.530 --> 00:37:24.650
The son, a student at West
Point had come home for a visit

00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:27.040
so the father decided that
it would be a good idea

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:29.770
for the family to see the statue together.

00:37:29.770 --> 00:37:31.890
And this is a quote from the father.

00:37:31.890 --> 00:37:33.710
It seemed like a good opportunity to talk

00:37:33.710 --> 00:37:35.670
about Korean history.

00:37:35.670 --> 00:37:38.270
When I asked him how he felt
after seeing the statue,

00:37:38.270 --> 00:37:40.867
he responded, "Compared
to when I read about it

00:37:40.867 --> 00:37:44.947
"in newspapers, seeing it
in person really moved it.

00:37:44.947 --> 00:37:48.177
"Articles make it seem
like someone else's story

00:37:48.177 --> 00:37:50.367
"but seeing the statue in person,

00:37:50.367 --> 00:37:52.877
"makes it seem like my story."

00:37:53.720 --> 00:37:56.290
I was struck by how the bronze girl statue

00:37:56.290 --> 00:37:58.900
made the comfort women
history more relevant

00:37:58.900 --> 00:38:01.080
and personal to Mr. O.

00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:03.720
Perhaps it was the lifelike
design of the statue

00:38:03.720 --> 00:38:06.470
as an adolescent girl
that resonated with him.

00:38:06.470 --> 00:38:08.370
Mr. O son and daughter also seemed

00:38:08.370 --> 00:38:10.430
to be moved by the experience.

00:38:10.430 --> 00:38:13.030
The son spoke of how the
statue brought history

00:38:13.030 --> 00:38:15.270
to life for him and made him feel

00:38:15.270 --> 00:38:17.480
a deeper connection to home.

00:38:17.480 --> 00:38:20.360
Quote, coming here and sitting
around the dinner table

00:38:20.360 --> 00:38:23.850
and my parents telling me
stories about their past,

00:38:23.850 --> 00:38:26.000
about their history, and coming out here,

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:28.490
brings it a lot closer to home, you know,

00:38:28.490 --> 00:38:31.560
being able to visually see

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:33.970
what they're talking about, end quote.

00:38:33.970 --> 00:38:36.170
Their sentiments were
similar to what people say

00:38:36.170 --> 00:38:38.830
about leading survivors in person

00:38:38.830 --> 00:38:42.360
or listening to their public testimonies.

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:44.120
The Glendale statue which is a replica

00:38:44.120 --> 00:38:46.320
of the memorial in Korea is located

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:50.920
on an open green facing west
towards a group of tall trees.

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:53.410
The statue and the chair
are elevated five inches

00:38:53.410 --> 00:38:55.460
off the ground on a stone block

00:38:55.460 --> 00:38:58.000
that sits in the middle
of a gravel clearing.

00:38:58.000 --> 00:39:00.780
The elevated position of the
memorial discourages people

00:39:00.780 --> 00:39:03.430
from sitting in the
chair, unlike the statue

00:39:03.430 --> 00:39:07.200
in Seoul, South Korea, across
from the Japanese embassy.

00:39:07.200 --> 00:39:10.110
This likely also encourages
people to treat the memorial

00:39:10.110 --> 00:39:12.020
like a shrine or grave.

00:39:12.020 --> 00:39:15.000
So instead of sartorial
performances of care

00:39:15.000 --> 00:39:16.850
which seem less necessary

00:39:16.850 --> 00:39:19.490
in Southern California's warmer weather,

00:39:19.490 --> 00:39:23.110
people in Glendale are more
likely to leave bouquets,

00:39:23.110 --> 00:39:26.883
potted plants and flowers
and vases around the statue.

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:31.090
The comfort memorials
in Seoul and Glendale

00:39:31.090 --> 00:39:33.630
are not simply objects
with static meanings.

00:39:33.630 --> 00:39:36.420
As scriptive things, these
memorials invite behaviors

00:39:36.420 --> 00:39:38.760
that illuminate the range of attachments

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:42.850
and feelings people have about
survivors and their cause.

00:39:42.850 --> 00:39:46.080
Through sartorial and
horticultural performances of care,

00:39:46.080 --> 00:39:49.870
individuals and communities
in Korea and the United States

00:39:49.870 --> 00:39:53.460
demonstrate how survivors
have touched people's hearts

00:39:53.460 --> 00:39:55.060
and motivated them to engage

00:39:55.060 --> 00:39:57.710
with a difficult historical past.

00:39:57.710 --> 00:40:00.000
Approaching Memorial
Building through the lens

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:03.030
of performance illuminates
the varied desires

00:40:03.030 --> 00:40:05.220
of Korean and Korean Americans

00:40:05.220 --> 00:40:07.890
that inform their commemorative practices.

00:40:07.890 --> 00:40:10.640
Those who dress the bronze
girl, care for the women

00:40:10.640 --> 00:40:12.530
who are sex slaves as young girls

00:40:12.530 --> 00:40:15.330
and genuinely want to honor the memory.

00:40:15.330 --> 00:40:19.770
Others see the memorials as
symbols of geopolitical issues

00:40:19.770 --> 00:40:21.980
they are committed to fighting for.

00:40:21.980 --> 00:40:25.420
Some see a Korea of
the past that they want

00:40:25.420 --> 00:40:27.230
to protect the memory of.

00:40:27.230 --> 00:40:30.600
And others, like the Japanese-American
activist in Glendale,

00:40:30.600 --> 00:40:33.090
connect the memorials to a larger fight

00:40:33.090 --> 00:40:35.870
to eradicate sex trafficking.

00:40:35.870 --> 00:40:38.440
Through interactions with
memorials such as performances

00:40:38.440 --> 00:40:42.380
of care, people become part
of trans-pacific communities

00:40:42.380 --> 00:40:43.660
of remembrance.

00:40:43.660 --> 00:40:48.230
They are commemorating not
only the wartime experiences

00:40:48.230 --> 00:40:52.370
of survivors but also
their brave activism.

00:40:52.370 --> 00:40:55.140
The act of sitting by the statues side,

00:40:55.140 --> 00:40:58.010
wrapping clothes around
it or leaving gifts,

00:40:58.010 --> 00:41:01.110
provide people with the
chance to participate

00:41:01.110 --> 00:41:03.580
in something larger than themselves

00:41:03.580 --> 00:41:06.190
and to share the comfort
women history with others

00:41:06.190 --> 00:41:07.920
while honoring the women.

00:41:07.920 --> 00:41:12.260
These performances of care
remind us of the vitality

00:41:12.260 --> 00:41:15.190
of everyday acts of redress.

00:41:15.190 --> 00:41:16.376
Thank you.

00:41:16.376 --> 00:41:19.376
(audience applause)

00:41:22.170 --> 00:41:24.730
- [Lady] Thank you for
your beautiful books

00:41:24.730 --> 00:41:26.353
and beautiful talks.

00:41:29.190 --> 00:41:31.580
I was noticing how the
two of you were thinking

00:41:31.580 --> 00:41:36.580
about trans-pacific as both
an imperial designation

00:41:37.540 --> 00:41:40.370
but also a site of contestation

00:41:40.370 --> 00:41:43.763
for alternative trans-pacific ties.

00:41:44.840 --> 00:41:47.370
And I was wondering if
you would comment on that

00:41:47.370 --> 00:41:50.278
and maybe think about the gendering

00:41:50.278 --> 00:41:53.900
of the trans-pacific in the
ways you're both using them

00:41:53.900 --> 00:41:55.730
in terms of soldiering on the one hand

00:41:55.730 --> 00:41:57.043
and care on the other.

00:42:03.530 --> 00:42:06.910
- I'm interested in thinking
about the trans-pacific

00:42:06.910 --> 00:42:09.580
in a very specific moment,

00:42:09.580 --> 00:42:14.400
of the post 1945 post-World
War II US projects in Asia

00:42:16.370 --> 00:42:20.020
and using the Vietnam War

00:42:20.020 --> 00:42:22.340
as a sight to think about the connections

00:42:22.340 --> 00:42:25.000
of all of these different
countries and territories

00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:28.583
that we otherwise don't
see the commonalities of.

00:42:30.140 --> 00:42:32.850
So certainly, an imperial project

00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:36.420
of trying to demonstrate how the US,

00:42:36.420 --> 00:42:41.420
even though it's trying hard
to separate and distance itself

00:42:41.630 --> 00:42:44.080
from the Japanese Empire of the past,

00:42:44.080 --> 00:42:46.020
it's still very much using the rhetoric

00:42:46.020 --> 00:42:48.880
of an Asia for Asians of the East Asia,

00:42:48.880 --> 00:42:52.160
co-prosperity sphere but at the same time,

00:42:52.160 --> 00:42:54.840
we do see that this is a project

00:42:54.840 --> 00:42:59.443
that fails in many ways in
the sense, that it mobilizes

00:43:02.010 --> 00:43:06.080
these unforeseen
alliances and interactions

00:43:06.080 --> 00:43:09.380
among different racialized
colonial subjects,

00:43:09.380 --> 00:43:12.980
not only about among
soldiers and civilians

00:43:12.980 --> 00:43:15.410
but base workers.

00:43:15.410 --> 00:43:17.960
So one thing that I don't
really dwell on in the book

00:43:17.960 --> 00:43:19.900
but it's certainly a part of this making

00:43:19.900 --> 00:43:23.320
of the decolonizing
Pacific is the alliances

00:43:23.320 --> 00:43:28.320
that we see between anti-war,
anti-military activists

00:43:29.010 --> 00:43:32.480
and sex workers at US
bases in the Philippines,

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:35.720
in Korea, in Okinawa and Japan.

00:43:35.720 --> 00:43:40.720
So in that sense, I see the
gendering of the trans-pacific

00:43:41.030 --> 00:43:45.340
is not only about creating it
like a masculinists project

00:43:46.180 --> 00:43:48.610
that's rooted in the labor of soldiering

00:43:48.610 --> 00:43:50.960
but it's also about how the military

00:43:50.960 --> 00:43:55.960
is mobilizing different
gender in very different ways.

00:43:58.380 --> 00:44:00.050
- And I'm thinking about the Pacific

00:44:00.050 --> 00:44:05.050
both and is a political,
social and cultural space.

00:44:05.100 --> 00:44:07.620
Thinking, first of all, in
terms of this trans-pacific

00:44:07.620 --> 00:44:08.880
as an imperial project.

00:44:08.880 --> 00:44:11.880
I followed though the
women and how in the years,

00:44:11.880 --> 00:44:13.740
leading up to and during World War II,

00:44:13.740 --> 00:44:16.690
if we track their movement
throughout the Pacific,

00:44:16.690 --> 00:44:19.250
it's fascinating to see decades later

00:44:19.250 --> 00:44:21.143
how the women, as activists,

00:44:22.070 --> 00:44:25.270
we circulate along
those paths but as a way

00:44:25.270 --> 00:44:27.270
to create a different kind of space,

00:44:27.270 --> 00:44:30.080
a space of potentiality for reimagining

00:44:31.000 --> 00:44:33.830
what redress looks like for the women.

00:44:33.830 --> 00:44:36.210
Today I didn't get a chance
to really talk about,

00:44:36.210 --> 00:44:38.840
in depth about theater
but what's fascinating

00:44:38.840 --> 00:44:41.140
about theater productions
about the comfort women

00:44:41.140 --> 00:44:45.840
is that many of them have a
transnational production history

00:44:45.840 --> 00:44:48.150
so for example, there is a play,

00:44:48.150 --> 00:44:50.420
the first Asian-American
play about the government

00:44:50.420 --> 00:44:52.340
in the United States by Chummy Kim,

00:44:52.340 --> 00:44:55.740
called comfort women was
picked up by a Korean director.

00:44:55.740 --> 00:44:57.890
She had read a review in Korean

00:44:57.890 --> 00:45:00.450
then contacted the playwright to translate

00:45:00.450 --> 00:45:02.550
and to bring this play to Korea.

00:45:02.550 --> 00:45:06.060
And it ended up having an
extended run throughout Korea

00:45:06.060 --> 00:45:08.350
and what's fascinating is how this play

00:45:08.350 --> 00:45:11.970
then also traveled from Korea to Canada.

00:45:11.970 --> 00:45:13.650
So for me, it's fascinating to think about

00:45:13.650 --> 00:45:17.060
not only the movements of the
women across different times

00:45:17.060 --> 00:45:19.780
but also how cultural producers

00:45:19.780 --> 00:45:22.890
are circulating between different spaces

00:45:22.890 --> 00:45:24.800
to create a redressive arena

00:45:24.800 --> 00:45:27.983
where people can re-engage with the issue.

00:45:30.184 --> 00:45:31.767
- [Lady] Thank you.

00:45:36.390 --> 00:45:38.070
- [Man] Thanks for these terrific papers,

00:45:38.070 --> 00:45:41.063
really eye-opening and moving.

00:45:42.640 --> 00:45:47.120
My question is about the Imperial move

00:45:47.120 --> 00:45:52.120
into pre-existing structures in Indochina,

00:45:52.160 --> 00:45:54.520
the French colonial structures,

00:45:54.520 --> 00:45:57.247
in the case of post-world War II,

00:45:57.247 --> 00:45:59.940
the movement of the United States military

00:45:59.940 --> 00:46:04.390
into the structures created
by the Japanese military,

00:46:04.390 --> 00:46:07.830
almost in some cases, if
I've read your work correctly

00:46:07.830 --> 00:46:12.820
was a seamless transition in the way

00:46:12.820 --> 00:46:15.590
in which that part of the history

00:46:15.590 --> 00:46:18.920
activates a relationship of colonialism

00:46:18.920 --> 00:46:20.490
with a deeper historical route

00:46:20.490 --> 00:46:23.450
and I just wondered if you'd
care to comment on that.

00:46:26.011 --> 00:46:30.243
- So in Japan, after
the end of World War II,

00:46:31.670 --> 00:46:34.080
there were various sex industries

00:46:34.080 --> 00:46:36.660
that were developed around military bases,

00:46:36.660 --> 00:46:38.860
one particular place in Okinawa.

00:46:38.860 --> 00:46:42.220
And what's interesting
about these structures

00:46:42.220 --> 00:46:45.240
that were built earlier
is that some of the women

00:46:45.240 --> 00:46:48.320
who were comfort women
during World War II,

00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:50.440
many of them decided not to return back

00:46:50.440 --> 00:46:51.520
to their home countries.

00:46:51.520 --> 00:46:54.630
So some of them ended up
staying at some of these camps

00:46:54.630 --> 00:46:56.430
and then serving US soldiers.

00:46:56.430 --> 00:46:58.650
And what's fascinating
is that in my research,

00:46:58.650 --> 00:47:02.150
I noticed that this
history has only come up

00:47:02.150 --> 00:47:05.270
when people talk about this
tribunal that was held in 2000,

00:47:05.270 --> 00:47:08.250
the women's international
war crimes tribunal

00:47:08.250 --> 00:47:10.840
which was a people's tribunal organized

00:47:10.840 --> 00:47:14.740
by various legal scholars and
women's advocacy organizations

00:47:14.740 --> 00:47:17.212
around the world to bring together 64

00:47:17.212 --> 00:47:18.770
comfort women survivors

00:47:18.770 --> 00:47:22.630
to redress the feelings
of the Tokyo tribunal.

00:47:22.630 --> 00:47:25.750
And what's fascinating is
that during that tribunal

00:47:25.750 --> 00:47:29.060
and especially, there
was the main tribunal

00:47:29.060 --> 00:47:30.130
and then there was a public hearing

00:47:30.130 --> 00:47:32.810
on contemporary acts of
violence against women

00:47:32.810 --> 00:47:34.670
is when they explicitly talked about

00:47:34.670 --> 00:47:39.030
the ongoing militarized violence
against women in Okinawa

00:47:39.030 --> 00:47:40.610
and it was during those presentations

00:47:40.610 --> 00:47:42.750
that legal scholars and activists

00:47:42.750 --> 00:47:44.950
were making these explicit connections

00:47:44.950 --> 00:47:47.980
between these different
structures of violence

00:47:47.980 --> 00:47:50.150
but it's interesting to
see how that history often

00:47:50.150 --> 00:47:54.320
is submerged and not discussed
in these different arenas

00:47:54.320 --> 00:47:57.880
of either theater or public testimony

00:47:57.880 --> 00:47:59.733
or memorial building.

00:48:00.810 --> 00:48:04.430
- So it's really interesting
to think about how the US

00:48:04.430 --> 00:48:09.430
is actively disavowing
its imperial projects

00:48:10.280 --> 00:48:12.050
and actively distancing itself

00:48:12.050 --> 00:48:15.540
from Japanese and European imperial powers

00:48:15.540 --> 00:48:17.840
before World War II, so one of the things

00:48:17.840 --> 00:48:22.400
that I'm trying to do in
this book is to really show

00:48:22.400 --> 00:48:27.130
the ways in which, contrary
to how Cold War histories

00:48:27.130 --> 00:48:31.980
are narrated, that 1945 really
did not mark a drastic break

00:48:31.980 --> 00:48:36.306
from the colonial, past
and the US post-colonial,

00:48:36.306 --> 00:48:39.023
present or post-World War II period.

00:48:40.140 --> 00:48:43.090
And one of the ways that
we see the continuation

00:48:44.075 --> 00:48:47.510
the ongoing legacies
of Japanese imperialism

00:48:47.510 --> 00:48:52.510
is in the desires of Korean subjects

00:48:53.600 --> 00:48:57.450
to join the military, many of the recruits

00:48:57.450 --> 00:49:02.450
who were recruited into
the Korean Constabulary

00:49:02.800 --> 00:49:06.520
after World War II were
people who had previously

00:49:06.520 --> 00:49:08.230
been trained or had been officers

00:49:08.230 --> 00:49:10.363
in the Japanese Imperial Army.

00:49:11.690 --> 00:49:15.120
So there's a continuation,
not only like in terms

00:49:15.120 --> 00:49:17.350
of structures, structures of colonialism

00:49:17.350 --> 00:49:19.900
but also in thinking about the desires

00:49:19.900 --> 00:49:21.953
of colonial subjects themselves.

00:49:31.200 --> 00:49:33.720
- [Lady] I have a
partially formed question.

00:49:33.720 --> 00:49:36.383
Oh, I'm sorry, somebody
else have their hand missed.

00:49:37.720 --> 00:49:41.180
No, I have a partially formed question

00:49:41.180 --> 00:49:44.310
that I think brings the work back

00:49:44.310 --> 00:49:47.780
into the United States proper in terms of,

00:49:47.780 --> 00:49:50.600
because so much of the work
is out there in the Pacific

00:49:50.600 --> 00:49:53.960
and we're tracing these different mappings

00:49:53.960 --> 00:49:55.750
that the two of you are
bringing to the field

00:49:55.750 --> 00:50:00.420
which are amazing but
I'm struck by Glendale

00:50:00.420 --> 00:50:03.500
and Southern California

00:50:03.500 --> 00:50:06.980
and I'm thinking about how is the work

00:50:06.980 --> 00:50:09.970
that you're doing helping
us we rethink something

00:50:09.970 --> 00:50:10.963
like Glendale.

00:50:12.390 --> 00:50:15.250
Is there a way in which that we can,

00:50:15.250 --> 00:50:18.030
as scholars in the field of
Asian-American studies need

00:50:18.030 --> 00:50:21.220
to think about the local
in a very different way,

00:50:21.220 --> 00:50:24.610
based on these mappings
that you're showing us.

00:50:24.610 --> 00:50:28.610
So I feel like that's what
you're partially doing for us

00:50:28.610 --> 00:50:31.403
and I wanted to hear a
little bit more about that.

00:50:37.060 --> 00:50:39.322
- Let's see, where to begin?

00:50:39.322 --> 00:50:40.600
(Simeon laughing)

00:50:40.600 --> 00:50:44.291
I think, for me, what's interesting is

00:50:44.291 --> 00:50:49.291
as a transnational Asian/American
performance scholar,

00:50:50.680 --> 00:50:54.720
it's fascinating when I look
at a place like Glendale

00:50:54.720 --> 00:50:58.860
because what I notice is
that I think to understand

00:50:58.860 --> 00:51:03.380
the phenomena of Memorial
Building in Glendale, California,

00:51:03.380 --> 00:51:06.260
I think we have to maybe
step back and think

00:51:06.260 --> 00:51:09.890
about a larger temporal time span

00:51:09.890 --> 00:51:12.910
because there's a way in
which the dedication ceremony,

00:51:12.910 --> 00:51:15.820
as I mentioned was, of
course, highly scripted,

00:51:15.820 --> 00:51:18.240
wanted to present this nonpartisan issue

00:51:18.240 --> 00:51:22.850
that people across the
board were joining hands for

00:51:22.850 --> 00:51:24.390
and wanting to support the women

00:51:24.390 --> 00:51:26.530
but what's fascinating
is that the organization

00:51:26.530 --> 00:51:31.530
behind the memorial was
also a number of individuals

00:51:33.100 --> 00:51:36.450
who are at the forefront of
the efforts in the states

00:51:36.450 --> 00:51:40.160
to pass HR 121 in the US
House of Representatives,

00:51:40.160 --> 00:51:44.420
which was the first resolution
passed in the United States,

00:51:44.420 --> 00:51:48.470
where the United States
called Japan to apologize

00:51:48.470 --> 00:51:51.630
and to acknowledge what
happened to the women.

00:51:51.630 --> 00:51:55.270
And what's fascinating about
that particular resolution

00:51:55.270 --> 00:51:58.480
is that it was not only a coalition

00:51:58.480 --> 00:52:01.320
of various Asian-American activists

00:52:01.320 --> 00:52:03.180
and organizations across the country

00:52:03.180 --> 00:52:05.470
but it was a transnational effort

00:52:05.470 --> 00:52:09.480
that also where individuals
here in the States collaborated

00:52:09.480 --> 00:52:12.100
with organizations in Korea to have some

00:52:12.100 --> 00:52:14.680
of the survivors come and testify

00:52:14.680 --> 00:52:17.500
before the US House of Representatives.

00:52:17.500 --> 00:52:19.660
So I think that it's important to think

00:52:19.660 --> 00:52:21.450
about the different histories behind

00:52:21.450 --> 00:52:25.010
the actual culminating events of Glendale

00:52:25.010 --> 00:52:27.150
but what's fascinating to me
is that I've been tracking

00:52:27.150 --> 00:52:28.610
what's been happening in Glendale.

00:52:28.610 --> 00:52:30.700
One of the fascinating things for me

00:52:30.700 --> 00:52:34.090
is that and I don't understand
Southern California climate

00:52:34.090 --> 00:52:37.590
but the bronze girl statue
is no longer bronze.

00:52:37.590 --> 00:52:41.330
She's now become rusted and
almost has a greenish hinge

00:52:42.270 --> 00:52:45.010
which is interesting and
it's just fascinating

00:52:45.010 --> 00:52:47.140
how that space has not only become a place

00:52:47.140 --> 00:52:49.750
where people come by and leave flowers

00:52:49.750 --> 00:52:52.710
on particular anniversaries
but it's now become a space

00:52:52.710 --> 00:52:54.240
for press conferences,

00:52:54.240 --> 00:52:58.350
whenever there's like a big
issue like the 2015 deal

00:52:58.350 --> 00:52:59.777
between the South Korean government

00:52:59.777 --> 00:53:00.860
and the Japanese government

00:53:00.860 --> 00:53:04.710
but it's also the space where
people will hold rituals

00:53:04.710 --> 00:53:07.840
and funereal like services for survivors

00:53:07.840 --> 00:53:10.020
who have passed away in Korea.

00:53:10.020 --> 00:53:12.770
So it's fascinating how
that very localized space

00:53:12.770 --> 00:53:16.190
of this park that's between
a senior citizens home

00:53:16.190 --> 00:53:19.760
and a public library, if we
look at it through the lens

00:53:19.760 --> 00:53:22.410
of performance, we get to see ways

00:53:22.410 --> 00:53:24.980
in which the space has this history

00:53:24.980 --> 00:53:27.540
that is connected not
only to ongoing politics

00:53:27.540 --> 00:53:29.840
in the States to advocate for these women

00:53:29.840 --> 00:53:32.820
but it's connected to
what's happening abroad.

00:53:32.820 --> 00:53:35.790
And for me, it's fascinating
to think about rituals

00:53:35.790 --> 00:53:39.310
and commemorating the women
long after they have passed away

00:53:39.310 --> 00:53:42.640
in relation to the
ongoing quest for redress

00:53:42.640 --> 00:53:43.920
for these women.

00:53:43.920 --> 00:53:47.680
So that's my long
roundabout way of answering

00:53:47.680 --> 00:53:49.580
your question about the ways in which

00:53:49.580 --> 00:53:51.583
that very localized space,

00:53:51.583 --> 00:53:56.260
it just has so many
fascinated sedimented meanings

00:53:56.260 --> 00:53:57.470
for national politics

00:53:57.470 --> 00:54:00.163
and the transnational politics of memory.

00:54:01.730 --> 00:54:04.740
- It's really interesting you
brought up Kathy and myself

00:54:04.740 --> 00:54:06.440
who was one of the speakers

00:54:06.440 --> 00:54:09.250
because she also appears in my project

00:54:09.250 --> 00:54:11.890
as an anti-war--
- Oh, get out!

00:54:11.890 --> 00:54:14.523
- Activists in the 1960s.

00:54:16.280 --> 00:54:19.820
So I guess to answer your question,

00:54:19.820 --> 00:54:24.820
I see both Asian-American
GIs and veterans,

00:54:27.699 --> 00:54:30.750
anti-war activists in Little
Tokyo in like wherever,

00:54:30.750 --> 00:54:33.160
in the United States really making sense

00:54:33.160 --> 00:54:36.460
of what was going on in
their local communities

00:54:36.460 --> 00:54:41.390
through the language of US imperialism

00:54:41.390 --> 00:54:43.860
and state violence in Vietnam.

00:54:43.860 --> 00:54:46.770
I mean that's a familiar story and

00:54:48.430 --> 00:54:51.420
the civil rights black power
Asian-American movements,

00:54:51.420 --> 00:54:54.460
the ways in which state
violence overseas became

00:54:54.460 --> 00:54:57.960
a way to really frame and
understand what was going on

00:54:57.960 --> 00:55:01.163
with urban renewal, with policing.

00:55:02.600 --> 00:55:04.920
But also I see

00:55:08.950 --> 00:55:12.210
domestic space, foreign space as really

00:55:12.210 --> 00:55:15.130
the boundaries of that,
being incredibly blurred

00:55:15.130 --> 00:55:18.133
and we definitely see that
in the case of Hawaii,

00:55:19.420 --> 00:55:23.932
where the militarized, racialized
training practices in Oahu

00:55:23.932 --> 00:55:27.120
had a direct correlation
to the kinds of violence

00:55:27.120 --> 00:55:28.860
in Vietnam.

00:55:28.860 --> 00:55:33.057
We can also talk about Camp
Pendleton in Southern California

00:55:33.057 --> 00:55:36.840
and the ways that again,

00:55:36.840 --> 00:55:38.710
the racialized training practice is there

00:55:38.710 --> 00:55:42.710
with the more recent wars,
had a direct relationship

00:55:42.710 --> 00:55:44.210
with the state violence overseas.

00:55:44.210 --> 00:55:49.210
So I see it's a different
way of making sense

00:55:50.460 --> 00:55:53.560
of what we think of as domestic spaces,

00:55:53.560 --> 00:55:58.193
that it's always tied to
Imperial projects elsewhere.

00:56:07.546 --> 00:56:12.040
- [Lady] Hi, I have a
question for Professor Son.

00:56:12.040 --> 00:56:15.820
I was really struck by your
reading of the empty chair

00:56:15.820 --> 00:56:18.690
in the bronze girl memorial as potentially

00:56:18.690 --> 00:56:21.410
an invitation to think about
solidarities and alliances

00:56:21.410 --> 00:56:24.090
that could be forged between
the comfort women movement

00:56:24.090 --> 00:56:27.050
and other sorts of
forms of state violence.

00:56:27.050 --> 00:56:29.950
I'm thinking about the
comfort women memorial

00:56:29.950 --> 00:56:32.710
that was recently
constructed in San Francisco

00:56:32.710 --> 00:56:35.610
that has three women holding hands.

00:56:35.610 --> 00:56:37.120
For those of you who are familiar,

00:56:37.120 --> 00:56:38.390
a Korean woman, a Chinese woman

00:56:38.390 --> 00:56:41.180
and I think a Filipino comfort woman

00:56:41.180 --> 00:56:43.610
which is a different
sort of way of disrupting

00:56:43.610 --> 00:56:45.560
that public memory.

00:56:45.560 --> 00:56:47.450
So I would love to hear your thoughts

00:56:47.450 --> 00:56:49.090
on the ways of these different forms

00:56:49.090 --> 00:56:53.700
of sculptural commemoration
invite different kinds of

00:56:54.770 --> 00:56:58.660
political possibilities that might exceed

00:56:58.660 --> 00:57:03.510
as well as bring back into focus the issue

00:57:03.510 --> 00:57:06.210
of comfort women because--

00:57:06.210 --> 00:57:07.550
- So to my knowledge,

00:57:07.550 --> 00:57:11.080
the San Francisco Comfort
Women Memorial, the trio,

00:57:11.080 --> 00:57:14.630
is the only comfort women
memorial that materializes

00:57:14.630 --> 00:57:17.200
this image of solidarity

00:57:17.200 --> 00:57:20.070
and what's fascinating about
that particular memorial

00:57:20.070 --> 00:57:23.030
is that it's located in Chinatown

00:57:23.030 --> 00:57:26.310
but it's in St. Mary's
Park which is a public park

00:57:26.310 --> 00:57:28.960
and it's fascinating to
see the coalition work

00:57:28.960 --> 00:57:31.320
across ethnic boundaries

00:57:31.320 --> 00:57:33.360
to make that particular
memorial fascinating

00:57:33.360 --> 00:57:36.493
and when I visited last year,

00:57:36.493 --> 00:57:38.750
what struck me about
that particular memorial

00:57:38.750 --> 00:57:42.090
is the ways in which it
invites the pedestrian

00:57:42.090 --> 00:57:45.340
to also move in this circular movement

00:57:45.340 --> 00:57:47.770
because you're walking around and also,

00:57:47.770 --> 00:57:50.100
most importantly looking up at the women

00:57:50.100 --> 00:57:52.670
in a position of veneration

00:57:52.670 --> 00:57:55.080
but what was fascinating
about the time when I visited

00:57:55.080 --> 00:57:57.200
is that if you looked across and I forget

00:57:57.200 --> 00:57:59.670
which direction it was
but across the street,

00:57:59.670 --> 00:58:02.080
there's an art school and on the wall,

00:58:02.080 --> 00:58:04.550
of the art school, maybe
you saw it, was a flag,

00:58:04.550 --> 00:58:07.000
the Imperial Japanese flag.

00:58:07.000 --> 00:58:10.670
So fascinating to think
about within that one space

00:58:10.670 --> 00:58:13.300
how you have different conflicting ideas

00:58:13.300 --> 00:58:15.100
around that particular time

00:58:15.100 --> 00:58:19.503
and what it means to
remember World War II.

00:58:20.570 --> 00:58:24.903
But I think that that
particular trio of memorials is,

00:58:26.950 --> 00:58:28.660
for me, what really struck
me about that memorial

00:58:28.660 --> 00:58:32.060
is not only is it a trio of
three different comfort women

00:58:32.060 --> 00:58:35.180
on a pedestal but there is a statue

00:58:35.180 --> 00:58:36.960
of Kim Hak-sun harmony,

00:58:36.960 --> 00:58:40.200
so she's the first Korean
woman who came forward

00:58:40.200 --> 00:58:43.340
on August 14, 1991 and what's fascinating

00:58:43.340 --> 00:58:48.040
is that she is standing and
she's looking at this trio.

00:58:48.040 --> 00:58:50.740
So it raises this interesting
question about time

00:58:50.740 --> 00:58:55.460
and also about the
intergenerational reckoning

00:58:55.460 --> 00:58:59.300
of this particular history
and that kind of duality

00:58:59.300 --> 00:59:02.600
between the young girls and
the older grandmother figure

00:59:02.600 --> 00:59:05.320
is something that is repeated
in the bronze girl statue

00:59:05.320 --> 00:59:09.300
that I talked about where
behind the bronze girl statue,

00:59:09.300 --> 00:59:12.660
you see the mosaic of an elderly woman

00:59:12.660 --> 00:59:16.610
and that iconography of the young girl

00:59:16.610 --> 00:59:19.920
and the grandmother figure has been used

00:59:19.920 --> 00:59:24.150
throughout the social movement,
particularly in South Korea

00:59:24.150 --> 00:59:26.350
in terms of the rhetoric
that's used at protests

00:59:26.350 --> 00:59:30.570
but also in the actual visual iconography

00:59:30.570 --> 00:59:33.283
in terms of images on banners.

00:59:39.970 --> 00:59:43.970
- Actually I have a question
for you, if you don't mind.

00:59:43.970 --> 00:59:48.053
- Yeah, it's interesting, you
and then I have a comment.

00:59:50.280 --> 00:59:55.280
- So I was really interested
also in the memorializations

00:59:55.320 --> 01:00:00.320
in the US and I was wondering
if there are locations

01:00:00.560 --> 01:00:03.290
in the US and Glendale and New Jersey

01:00:03.290 --> 01:00:05.023
invited a kind of,

01:00:06.920 --> 01:00:10.730
a different kind of critique
among Korean-Americans

01:00:10.730 --> 01:00:14.750
about the role of the US in perpetuating

01:00:14.750 --> 01:00:19.690
the system of sexual slavery
like after World War II,

01:00:19.690 --> 01:00:22.530
like if that contradiction
of having these memorials

01:00:22.530 --> 01:00:25.723
in the US ever came up.

01:00:27.320 --> 01:00:31.690
- So the first memorial on
public land on the East Coast

01:00:31.690 --> 01:00:34.090
was in Palisades Park, New Jersey

01:00:34.090 --> 01:00:36.400
outside of the public library

01:00:36.400 --> 01:00:40.790
and in my interviews with
various people involved

01:00:40.790 --> 01:00:42.570
in that particular memorial project

01:00:42.570 --> 01:00:45.213
and also my attendance
at a dedication ceremony,

01:00:47.020 --> 01:00:50.140
people did not express
this kind of criticism

01:00:50.140 --> 01:00:51.823
and indictment of the US.

01:00:52.900 --> 01:00:55.430
And it did not surprise
me, that of course,

01:00:55.430 --> 01:00:57.700
in the dedication ceremony,
that would not be expressed

01:00:57.700 --> 01:01:00.950
because part of the possibility
of even having the memorial

01:01:00.950 --> 01:01:04.010
on public land meant that
there was coalition building

01:01:04.010 --> 01:01:07.223
between Korean Americans and non-Koreans.

01:01:08.340 --> 01:01:12.690
But what I found fascinating
about that particular memorial

01:01:12.690 --> 01:01:17.690
is the backlash, how a number
of Japanese politicians came

01:01:18.810 --> 01:01:21.830
to visit the mayor, Mayor Rotundo

01:01:21.830 --> 01:01:23.890
to say what these people are saying,

01:01:23.890 --> 01:01:26.250
it's historically false.

01:01:26.250 --> 01:01:28.230
Why would this little city get involved

01:01:28.230 --> 01:01:30.040
in this particular issue,

01:01:30.040 --> 01:01:32.310
we wanna give you all
these gifts of cherry trees

01:01:32.310 --> 01:01:34.300
if you will remove the Memorial.

01:01:34.300 --> 01:01:36.497
And the mayor famously said,

01:01:36.497 --> 01:01:38.367
"You can keep your cherry trees.

01:01:38.367 --> 01:01:42.590
"We're keeping our Memorial
and we did our research."

01:01:42.590 --> 01:01:45.440
So it's fascinating how
this particular memorial

01:01:45.440 --> 01:01:47.680
has taken on this life in this community

01:01:47.680 --> 01:01:49.830
because after that particular incident,

01:01:49.830 --> 01:01:51.830
then and it was reported and of course,

01:01:51.830 --> 01:01:54.010
a number of different Korean newspapers,

01:01:54.010 --> 01:01:58.050
that unleashed this
memorial building effort

01:01:58.050 --> 01:01:58.883
across the states

01:01:58.883 --> 01:02:01.760
where different
Korean-American organizations

01:02:01.760 --> 01:02:04.710
were saying, we're gonna
build our own memorial

01:02:04.710 --> 01:02:06.900
but interestingly enough,
this kind of critique,

01:02:06.900 --> 01:02:09.470
I have not encountered as much during

01:02:09.470 --> 01:02:13.480
these public ceremonies
but I find this critique

01:02:13.480 --> 01:02:15.423
in cultural work like theater,

01:02:16.280 --> 01:02:18.030
that this theater becomes the space

01:02:18.030 --> 01:02:20.610
where people are able to talk more

01:02:20.610 --> 01:02:24.900
about not just US
complicity in what happened

01:02:24.900 --> 01:02:27.800
but also are able to talk
about Korean complicity

01:02:27.800 --> 01:02:29.303
and what happened.

01:02:31.440 --> 01:02:34.360
So for me, just the
comment and the connection

01:02:34.360 --> 01:02:37.400
is it's fascinating that
the comfort women survivors

01:02:37.400 --> 01:02:39.870
have become advocates for other women

01:02:39.870 --> 01:02:41.920
who are survivors of
gender-based violence.

01:02:41.920 --> 01:02:44.680
So two survivors started something
called the Butterfly Fund

01:02:44.680 --> 01:02:48.690
in 2013 where when they
would go and testify,

01:02:48.690 --> 01:02:51.770
they would collect funds
and different organizations

01:02:51.770 --> 01:02:54.750
would raise money so these
women could support victims

01:02:54.750 --> 01:02:57.790
of other gender-based violence
and one group of women

01:02:57.790 --> 01:03:01.340
they supported were Vietnamese
women who were survivors

01:03:01.340 --> 01:03:03.880
of sexual violence
committed by Korean soldiers

01:03:03.880 --> 01:03:06.350
during the Vietnam War
and what's fascinating

01:03:06.350 --> 01:03:08.810
is that a number of the
comfort women activists

01:03:08.810 --> 01:03:12.210
from the main organization
have been going to Vietnam

01:03:12.210 --> 01:03:15.340
to collect public testimony
and what's fascinating

01:03:15.340 --> 01:03:17.210
is that they're using
some of the same rhetoric

01:03:17.210 --> 01:03:19.070
they used to advocate for redress

01:03:19.070 --> 01:03:22.690
from the Japanese government
to now advocate for redress

01:03:22.690 --> 01:03:25.970
for these Vietnamese women
from the Korean government

01:03:25.970 --> 01:03:30.770
and just fascinating to see
the kind of entanglement

01:03:30.770 --> 01:03:33.160
between these different histories
of gender-based violence

01:03:33.160 --> 01:03:35.210
but ways in which also it's not just

01:03:35.210 --> 01:03:36.630
in the structures of violence,

01:03:36.630 --> 01:03:41.350
it's also in the different
modes of political organizing.

01:03:41.350 --> 01:03:43.140
- Yeah, I remember seeing that exhibit

01:03:43.140 --> 01:03:47.240
at the Women's Human
Rights Museum in Seoul

01:03:47.240 --> 01:03:51.245
where the last exhibit at the
time was a special exhibit

01:03:51.245 --> 01:03:54.770
devoted to this issue and I
thought it was so powerful

01:03:54.770 --> 01:03:59.770
that it expanded the
issue and also critiqued

01:04:00.190 --> 01:04:02.633
Korean state violence.

01:04:09.250 --> 01:04:11.470
- [Mary] I think this is the really...

01:04:11.470 --> 01:04:14.010
This is a treat to get to see
you two talk to each other.

01:04:14.010 --> 01:04:16.420
So I think this is a great place to end.

01:04:16.420 --> 01:04:18.250
Let's give a wonderful round of applause.

01:04:18.250 --> 01:04:21.250
(everyone applauds)

01:04:25.627 --> 01:04:28.210
(gentle music)

